{
  "title": "Exercise 4.2 — Question 21",
  "type": "PLAYER",
  "body": {
    "version": "deck-v2",
    "name": "Exercise 4.2 — Question 21",
    "audio": "music.mp3",
    "theme": "blue",
    "background": {
      "backgroundColor": "#010214",
      "backgroundImage": "banner.webp",
      "backgroundImageOpacity": 0.09
    },
    "deck": [
      {
        "type": "eq",
        "start": 0,
        "end": 90,
        "data": [
          {
            "name": "line",
            "type": "heading",
            "content": "Exercise 4.2 — Question 21",
            "showAt": 0,
            "spItems": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "content": "The expression a⁶ + 729 can be written in two ways."
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "content": "Which form should we use for factorization, and why?"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "name": "line",
            "type": "math",
            "content": "a^6+729",
            "showAt": 10,
            "spItems": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "content": "Since a⁶=(a³)² and 729=27², it is a sum of two squares."
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "content": "But the sum of two squares has no standard factorization formula."
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "name": "line",
            "type": "math",
            "content": "a^6+729=(a^2)^3+9^3",
            "showAt": 20,
            "spItems": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "content": "Also, a⁶=(a²)³ and 729=9³."
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "content": "So the expression is a sum of two cubes."
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "name": "line",
            "type": "heading",
            "content": "Use the sum of cubes",
            "showAt": 30,
            "spItems": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "content": "The sum of cubes has the identity a³+b³=(a+b)(a²−ab+b²)."
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "content": "This gives us a direct factorization."
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "name": "line",
            "type": "math",
            "content": "(a^2+9)(a^4-9a^2+81)",
            "showAt": 40,
            "spItems": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "content": "Take the two cubes as a² and 9."
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "content": "Substitute them into the sum of cubes formula."
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "name": "line",
            "type": "heading",
            "content": "Why this form?",
            "showAt": 50,
            "spItems": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "content": "The sum-of-squares form does not provide the required school-level factorization."
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "content": "The sum-of-cubes form gives the factorization immediately."
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "name": "line",
            "type": "math",
            "content": "a^6+729=(a^2+9)(a^4-9a^2+81)",
            "showAt": 60,
            "spItems": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "content": "This is the required factorization."
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "content": "The second factor has no further integer factorization."
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}